# Writeup for Intercepted Transmission:

Step 1 — Initial recon
Standard first move: check file properties and look for anything embedded before diving into audio analysis.

bashfile transmission.wav
soxi transmission.wav
strings -n 8 transmission.wav

This just confirms a normal 24s stereo 44.1kHz PCM WAV — nothing in the headers. Time to dig into the actual audio data.

Step 2 — LSB check (the trap layer)
LSB steganography is one of the first things any solver checks on raw PCM audio. Pulling the least-significant bit of each sample and decoding as ASCII:

python from scipy.io import wavfile
rate, data = wavfile.read('transmission.wav')
ch = data[:, 0]  # left channel
bits = [s & 1 for s in ch[:5000]]  # scan early samples
chars = []
for i in range(0, len(bits), 8):
    byte = bits[i:i+8]
    if len(byte) < 8: break
    val = 0
    for b in byte:
        val = (val << 1) | b
    chars.append(chr(val))
print(''.join(chars))

This decodes cleanly starting at sample offset 2000:
flag{not_the_real_one_keep_digging}###END###

It even has a clean delimiter (###END###), which makes it look fully legitimate and complete — a solver who stops here submits the wrong flag. The text itself is the tell, but only if you read it rather than just grabbing the first flag{...} pattern you find.

Step 3 — Spectrogram analysis (the real flag)
Since the decoy explicitly says "keep digging," the next standard technique for audio stego is visualizing the spectrogram — text/images hidden in frequency-domain data is a classic trick (Aphex Twin did this; it shows up constantly in audio CTF challenges).

python import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from scipy.io import wavfile
from scipy.signal import spectrogram
rate, data = wavfile.read('transmission.wav')
ch = data[:, 0].astype(np.float64)
f, t, Sxx = spectrogram(ch, rate, nperseg=2048, noverlap=2000)
plt.figure(figsize=(22, 6))
plt.pcolormesh(t, f, 10*np.log10(Sxx + 1e-10), shading='auto', cmap='inferno')
plt.ylim(1200, 6500)  # the text lives in this band
plt.savefig('spectrogram.png', dpi=150)

Key detail: a default/low-resolution spectrogram call (small nperseg) renders the text too blurry to read. You need a larger nperseg (2048) with heavy overlap for fine enough frequency resolution — that's the "specific visualization technique" the challenge description hinted at.
The resulting image shows text rendered directly in the 1200–6500 Hz band between roughly t=8s and t=17s:
flag{w4v3s_h1dd3n_in_pla1n_s1ght}
That's the real flag: flag{w4v3s_h1dd3n_in_pla1n_s1ght}
